                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Read the team`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `You inherit Atlas mid-flight, six weeks from a client acceptance milestone whose slip costs <strong>€180k</strong> and threatens a <strong>€2.4M renewal</strong>. The pulse is blunt: engagement 71, meaningfulness 6.1/10, turnover risk elevated for 3 of 8. No one hands you a leadership prescription. Read the people first: the star isn't lazy and the underperformer isn't slacking — diagnose the <em>root</em>, not the symptom. Then spend your eight scarce one-on-one hours where the leading indicators actually live, not evenly to feel fair.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Empower or direct: the star and the squad`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `The first real choices. Your disengaging star carries the critical path. Van Dierendonck's model bites here: <strong>empowerment, stewardship and humility</strong> rebuild a follower's meaning — give them ownership of the hardest module and clear their blockers, or tighten control and reassign the path. And under deadline pressure, does the <strong>~40 hours of promised development</strong> for your two high-potentials survive? Cancelling it strips a job resource in the exact week the pulse flags them as flight risks. An expert and two high-potentials do not need the same move.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — The underperformer and the deadline`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Three weeks out, the once-solid engineer misses a fourth commitment. The directive instinct is a performance plan today; the servant-leadership move is to understand the <em>caregiving situation at home</em>, co-design a realistic short-term load <strong>with clear standards</strong>, and protect well-being while holding the bar. This is where van Dierendonck's upward spiral lives — the right move puts a struggling member back into <strong>flow</strong>; pressure-only spirals the whole team down. Then concentrate the squad's remaining capacity on the milestone — too little slips it, too much burns the people you just protected.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Land the milestone, hold the team`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The final two weeks, then you present to a Polder partner. The milestone outcome, the star's stay/leave decision, and the squad's engagement are now resolved by your accumulated behaviour. Run the push as an all-out crunch and you buy a few performance points while spiralling morale down; run it <strong>sustainably</strong> and you trust the spiral you built. The partner will probe the trade-off: <em>“You hit the date — did you keep the team you'll need for the renewal?”</em> Meaningfulness was supposed to convert to <strong>performance</strong>, not replace it. Tell the honest story.`,

                    r1_root_label:          `Diagnose the ROOT of the star's disengagement (not the symptom).`,
                    r1_under_label:         `Diagnose the ROOT of the underperformer's slip.`,
                    r1_oneonone_label:      `Spend your scarce one-on-one time this round (total must be 8 h)`,
                    r1_total_readout:       `One-on-one hours allocated:`,
                    r1_total_cap:           `/ 8 h`,
                    root_meaning:           `They have lost ownership and purpose — the hardest module became a grind with no voice in it; a meaning and autonomy problem`,
                    root_lazy:              `They have checked out and stopped caring — camera off, terse, declining work; treat it as an attitude problem`,
                    root_pay:               `It is purely about money — they want a raise and are leveraging the milestone; sort the comp later`,
                    root_unknown:           `Impossible to know without asking — but the deadline means I act on the symptom now and ask later`,
                    under_life:             `Something changed outside work — a previously solid engineer rarely slips for no reason; understand the cause before judging`,
                    under_skill:            `A skills gap — they can no longer keep up technically; plan a performance improvement track`,
                    under_discipline:       `A discipline problem — they have gone slack; tighten oversight and set a warning`,
                    under_noroot:           `The cause does not matter — a missed commitment is a missed commitment; the bar is the bar`,
                    alloc_star:             `The disengaging star — listen for what ownership and meaning they have lost`,
                    alloc_under:            `The underperformer — understand what changed before deciding anything`,
                    alloc_hipo:             `The two high-potentials — hear what the development promise means to them`,
                    alloc_rest:             `The rest of the squad — the four steady members watching how you show up`,

                    r2_star_label:          `The disengaging star — pick your move along the empowering↔directive spectrum.`,
                    r2_dev_label:           `The development promise to your two high-potentials, under deadline pressure.`,
                    r2_blocker_label:       `How much of your own week do you spend removing blockers vs. adding oversight? (% on removing blockers)`,
                    r2_blocker_readout:     `Week spent removing blockers (vs. control):`,
                    r2_pct:                 `%`,
                    r2_blocker_hint:        `Servant leadership stands back and clears the path; pressure-only leadership adds check-ins. The expert needs the former, not the latter.`,
                    star_empower:           `Empower — give them ownership of the hardest module, surface why it matters, remove their blockers, restore voice`,
                    star_support:           `Support lightly — a kind chat and more check-ins, but keep the critical path where it is`,
                    star_direct:            `Direct — reassign the critical path off them and increase check-ins to protect the deadline`,
                    dev_protect:            `Protect it — keep the ~40 hours of growth and coaching time; a job resource the pulse says they need now`,
                    dev_halve:              `Halve it — keep some development, redirect the rest into delivery as a compromise`,
                    dev_cancel:             `Cancel it — reclaim all ~40 hours straight into the milestone; growth can wait six weeks`,

                    r3_perf_label:          `The underperformer missed a fourth commitment — act on the performance dilemma.`,
                    r3_standard_label:      `Where do you set the bar in that conversation?`,
                    r3_capacity_label:      `Allocate the squad's remaining capacity for the milestone push (% onto the critical path).`,
                    r3_capacity_readout:    `Capacity concentrated on the critical path:`,
                    r3_capacity_hint:       `Concentrate too little and the milestone slips; concentrate everything and you burn the squad and the high-potentials in the final push. Balance demand with the resources you protected.`,
                    perf_carehold:          `Care and hold — understand the caregiving situation, co-design a realistic short-term load WITH clear standards and support`,
                    perf_plan:              `Formal plan now — move straight to a performance improvement plan; it protects delivery optics`,
                    perf_ignore:            `Keep trusting them — say nothing, let the team self-correct; raising it would feel harsh`,
                    standard_clear:         `Clear and humane — a reduced but real commitment they can hit, with explicit standards and a check-in cadence`,
                    standard_drop:          `Drop it — remove their commitments entirely until things settle; pure compassion, no expectation`,
                    standard_ignore:        `Unchanged — full load regardless of what is happening at home; the deadline does not flex`,

                    r4_finalpush_label:     `Final two weeks — how do you run the milestone push?`,
                    r4_partner_label:       `Present the outcome to the Polder partner — what story do you tell?`,
                    r4_philosophy_label:    `State your operating philosophy as a leader.`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly will you hold that meaningfulness must convert to performance, not replace it? (%)`,
                    push_sustainable:       `Sustainable push — focus, protect well-being, keep the conditions for flow; trust the spiral you built`,
                    push_crunch:            `All-out crunch — long hours for everyone, ship at any cost; recover the team afterwards`,
                    push_protect:           `Protect first — ease the pace to keep everyone happy even if the date is at risk`,
                    partner_tradeoff:       `Lead with the trade-off — name the milestone outcome AND the team you kept for the renewal, honestly`,
                    partner_datebrag:       `Lead with the date — "we hit the milestone"; do not dwell on how the team is feeling`,
                    partner_feelings:       `Lead with morale — "the team loves working here now"; downplay the delivery position`,
                    phil_servant:           `Serve and hold — create meaning and clear blockers, AND hold a humane standard; care and accountability together`,
                    phil_results:           `Results first — people follow when you deliver; pressure is how good teams perform`,
                    phil_harmony:           `Harmony first — happy people deliver; avoid hard conversations to keep the peace`,

                    warn_ooo_negative:      `One-on-one hours cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_ooo_total:         `Your one-on-one hours must total exactly 8 — that is the time you have this round. Re-balance.`,
                    warn_blocker_range:     `The blocker-removal share must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_capacity_range:    `The capacity concentration must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_root:          `<strong>That's the right read.</strong> A strong engineer going camera-off and terse is rarely a lazy one — they've usually lost ownership and purpose in the work. Name it as a meaning-and-autonomy problem and Round 2 has a real move to make: give the ownership back.`,
                    coach_r1_unknown:       `<strong>“I'll act on the symptom now and ask later” is the deadline talking.</strong> Acting on a misread of your strongest engineer is exactly how a recoverable disengagement becomes an exit. The two-week clock is real — but five minutes of genuine listening changes which move you make.`,
                    coach_r1_lazy:          `<strong>Calling it an attitude problem is the symptom, not the root.</strong> Treat a disengaging expert as “checked out and lazy” and your Round 2 move will be control — which is exactly what deepens the disengagement. Diagnose the meaning loss first.`,
                    coach_r1_pay:           `<strong>It is rarely just the money.</strong> Comp matters, but a star who's gone quiet and declines stretch work is usually telling you about ownership and meaning, not salary. Solve the wrong problem and the raise won't keep them.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You read the star's loss of meaning AND refused to assume the underperformer simply went slack — a previously solid engineer rarely slips for no reason. That's the servant-leadership lens: cause before judgement, on both. Now your one-on-one time can actually do something.`,
                    coach_r1_discipline:    `<strong>“They've gone slack” skips the question that matters.</strong> A previously reliable engineer who suddenly misses commitments usually has a reason — and the reason here is caregiving at home. Tighten oversight on that and you'll signal to the whole squad that struggle gets punished.`,
                    coach_r1_noroot:        `<strong>“The bar is the bar, cause doesn't matter” is Common Error number four.</strong> Holding a standard and understanding the cause are not opposites — the servant-leadership move does both. Skip the cause and you escalate a caregiving situation into a performance plan.`,
                    coach_r1_thinspread:    `<strong>Spreading your hours evenly to feel fair starves the three fronts that are actually on fire.</strong> The star, the underperformer and the high-potentials are where the leading indicators live this round. Even attention everywhere is no attention where it counts.`,
                    coach_r2_empower:       `<strong>That's the empowering move an expert needs.</strong> Giving the star ownership of the hardest module, surfacing its purpose and clearing their blockers rebuilds meaning — and meaning is what keeps them. You've started the upward spiral on your strongest, most critical engineer.`,
                    coach_r2_support:       `<strong>A kind chat is better than control, but it's not enough for an expert.</strong> More check-ins without restored ownership reads as monitoring, not trust. The disengagement is about lost meaning — light support doesn't give the meaning back.`,
                    coach_r2_direct:        `<strong>You reassigned the critical path and doubled the check-ins.</strong> Velocity may tick up this week — but you just told your most critical engineer you don't trust them, in the exact week they were already disengaging. That's the downward spiral starting on the one person who can blow the milestone.`,
                    coach_r2_oversight:     `<strong>Almost all oversight, almost no blocker-clearing.</strong> Standing back and removing obstacles is the servant-leadership behaviour an expert responds to; piling on check-ins is the opposite. You picked a good stance and then ran it like a manager watching a junior.`,
                    coach_r2_devcancel:     `<strong>You reclaimed ~40 hours by cancelling development — straight into the milestone.</strong> You also broke a promise to your two highest-potential members in the exact week the pulse flagged them as flight risks. You may have bought six weeks of hours and triggered two exits worth more than the milestone. What did the trade really cost?`,
                    coach_r3_carehold:      `<strong>That's performance management with care.</strong> Understanding the caregiving cause, co-designing a load they can actually hit, and keeping a clear standard holds the bar AND protects the person. Done right, this is exactly the move that puts a struggling engineer back into flow — the upward spiral van Dierendonck describes.`,
                    coach_r3_plan:          `<strong>Straight to a performance plan — decisive, and wrong here.</strong> You never asked why a solid engineer slipped, and the reason is caregiving at home. You've now signalled to the whole squad that struggle gets punished, not supported. There was a version that held the standard AND understood the cause. Why did you skip it?`,
                    coach_r3_abdicate:      `<strong>Trusting the team to self-correct on a fourth missed commitment is abdication, not empowerment.</strong> Servant leadership is not the absence of accountability — standing back without standards is just standing back. The rest of the squad is watching you not act, and learning that the bar doesn't apply.`,
                    coach_r3_dropbar:       `<strong>Removing every commitment is compassion without accountability.</strong> Protecting well-being doesn't mean zero expectation — it means a real but reachable one. Drop the bar entirely and you've helped neither the person (who loses their footing) nor the milestone.`,
                    coach_r3_holdrigid:     `<strong>Full load regardless of the caregiving situation is the deadline talking, not leadership.</strong> Holding a standard humanely means flexing the load while keeping the standard — not pretending nothing changed at home. Rigid here and you'll lose the person and the trust of everyone watching.`,
                    coach_r3_undercap:      `<strong>You've under-concentrated on the critical path.</strong> Meaning and well-being matter — but the upward spiral is supposed to deliver performance, and the milestone won't land itself. Too little focus here and you'll keep a happy team and still invoke the penalty.`,
                    coach_r3_overcap:       `<strong>You've poured everything onto the critical path.</strong> You may move the performance number, but you're burning the squad — and the two high-potentials you just protected — in the final push. Demand without resources is exactly the spiral you were avoiding.`,
                    coach_r4_sustainable:   `<strong>A sustainable push is the spiral paying off.</strong> Focus and protected well-being convert the meaning you built into delivery — performance and engagement rising together is precisely what van Dierendonck's upward spiral predicts. You're landing the date without spending the team.`,
                    coach_r4_crunch:        `<strong>All-out crunch buys a few performance points and spends the team.</strong> Long hours for everyone in the final fortnight erodes the meaning and well-being you spent three rounds building — and a crunch-exhausted squad is a flight-risk squad right when you need them for the renewal.`,
                    coach_r4_protectonly:   `<strong>Easing the pace to keep everyone happy may miss the date.</strong> The upward spiral is supposed to deliver performance, not just good feelings. A beloved team that invokes a €180k penalty is not the win it feels like — meaning has to translate into output.`,
                    coach_r4_mature:        `<strong>That's mature leadership.</strong> A sustainable push plus an honest trade-off story to the partner — naming the milestone outcome AND the team you kept — is exactly the integrated frame. You didn't optimise the date in one room and morale in another; you reconciled them.`,
                    coach_r4_feelings:      `<strong>Leading with “the team loves it here” and downplaying delivery is half the story.</strong> The partner is asking about the renewal, which needs the date AND the team. A morale-only story to someone holding a €2.4M account reads as avoiding the question.`,
                    coach_r4_resultsonly:   `<strong>“Pressure is how good teams perform” is the philosophy that started your downward spiral.</strong> Results-first as a creed strips the meaning that drives durable performance. It works for one sprint and costs you the people you need for the next one.`,
                    coach_r4_harmonyonly:   `<strong>“Avoid hard conversations to keep the peace” is empowerment-as-abdication as a creed.</strong> Harmony without accountability feels kind and quietly tells your best people the bar doesn't matter. Servant leadership keeps the peace by holding the standard humanely, not by avoiding it.`,
                    coach_onesize:          `<strong>You ran the same playbook on all three.</strong> The same stance for a disengaging expert, two high-potentials and a struggling junior — but they need different things. Servant leadership reads the person. Where did one-size-fits-all just cost you?`,
                    coach_starexit:         `<strong>Your star just handed in notice.</strong> Camera-off became a resignation: the meaning kept eroding, the risk kept climbing, and the one engineer on the critical path is gone. That's a 3–4 week slip and ~€95k to backfill and re-ramp — the exit you could see coming in Round 1 is now the milestone you'll miss.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Read both roots correctly; invested the one-on-one time where it counts.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Mis-diagnosed a root or spread the one-on-one time too thin.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Empowered the expert; kept the development promise.`,
                    sum_r2_directive:       `Directive on the star and cancelled development — control under pressure.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Half-measures on the star or the development promise.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Held the bar humanely; cause before escalation.`,
                    sum_r3_punitive:       `Escalated without diagnosing the cause.`,
                    sum_r3_abdicate:        `Avoided the underperformer — accountability slipped.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Mixed signal on standards or capacity.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Sustainable push; honest trade-off; serve-and-hold philosophy.`,
                    sum_r4_crunch:          `All-out crunch — bought delivery, spent the team.`,
                    sum_r4_soft:            `Protected feelings at the milestone's expense.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Final push under-converted meaning into performance.`,

                    insight_servant:        `Servant leader: you read each person, empowered the expert, kept the development promise, held the underperformer's standard with care, and ran a sustainable final push. The milestone landed and you kept the team you'll need for the renewal — the upward spiral delivered performance, not just good feelings.`,
                    insight_starexit:       `The star exited. The disengagement you could see in Round 1 kept deepening — control where there should have been ownership, or pressure where there should have been meaning — until your most critical engineer left, slipping the milestone 3–4 weeks and costing ~€95k to backfill. The leading indicator was screaming; the exit was avoidable.`,
                    insight_slipped:        `The milestone slipped and the client invoked the €180k penalty. Somewhere the performance index never recovered — too little focus at the critical path, an exit you didn't prevent, or a downward spiral that quietly drained delivery. Meaningfulness was supposed to convert to output, and here it didn't.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed outcome. You held some of the team and some of the date, but the three dilemmas never fully cohered into one servant-leadership stance — there's a seam where care and accountability didn't quite meet.`,
                    insight_devbroken:      `You broke the development promise. Cancelling growth time for two high-potentials in the week the pulse flagged them as flight risks stripped a job resource at peak demand — exactly the resources-vs-demands trap, and a signal the whole squad read.`,
                    insight_spiraldown:     `You triggered the downward spiral: pressure-only leadership eroded meaning, which eroded performance, which invited more pressure. Van Dierendonck's warning made concrete — control bought a sprint and cost the engine that drives durable delivery.`,
                    insight_spiralup:       `You started the upward spiral: meaning and the conditions for flow lifted engagement and performance together, which deepened meaning further. That self-reinforcing loop — not heroics in the final fortnight — is what landed this.`,
                    insight_onesize:        `You applied one fixed style to all three dilemmas. A disengaging expert, two high-potentials and a struggling junior needed different moves; the uniform stance cost you on at least one. Servant leadership reads the person, not the playbook.`,
                    insight_pyrrhic:        `You hit the date but didn't keep the team. The partner's question lands hard: the renewal needs the people, and a squad with high turnover risk after a milestone is a squad you may not have for the next one. A Pyrrhic delivery.`,
                    insight_meaningnoperf:  `Meaningfulness is high but performance lagged — the team feels great and the work didn't fully land. The upward spiral is meant to deliver output; meaning that doesn't convert is only half the model.`,

                    hint_starexit:          `Your star has exited — the milestone is now badly exposed. The lesson is upstream: ownership and meaning in Rounds 1–2 were the move that would have kept them.`,
                    hint_spiraldown:        `You've tipped into a downward spiral — pressure eroding meaning eroding performance. Shift toward empowerment and a humane standard before it compounds further.`,
                    hint_devbroken:         `You cancelled promised development for two flight-risk high-potentials. Watch the turnover risk — a broken promise under pressure is read loudly by the whole squad.`,
                    hint_risk:              `Turnover risk is dangerously high. Meaning and ownership are the leading indicators — restore them now, before the risk becomes an exit you can't reverse.`,
                    hint_perf:              `The performance index is low with little time left. Concentrate capacity on the critical path — but not at the cost of burning the people who have to deliver it.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — engagement, performance and low turnover risk all healthy. Hold the serve-and-hold line through the final push and land it sustainably.`,

